  "concept": "outlier detection with the IQR rule inside a pandas groupby",
  "task": "A ₹5,000 payment is normal in Mumbai but may be unusual in a small town, so outliers should be found **within each group**.\nThe IQR rule: Q1 = 25th percentile, Q3 = 75th percentile, IQR = Q3 − Q1. A value is an outlier if it is below Q1 − 1.5×IQR or above Q3 + 1.5×IQR.\nFirst write `iqr_bounds(values)` that takes a pandas Series and returns `(lower, upper)` using `values.quantile(0.25)` and `values.quantile(0.75)`.\nThen write `outliers_by_group(df, group_col, value_col)` that returns only the rows of `df` that are outliers **within their own group**, keeping the original index and all columns. `df.groupby(group_col)[value_col].transform(some_function)` runs your function on each group and lines the answers up with the original rows.\nExample: `iqr_bounds(pd.Series([10, 20, 30, 40, 1000]))` is `(-10.0, 70.0)`. In `make_payments()` the ₹20,000 Mumbai payment and the ₹5,000 Indore payment are outliers, but the ₹5,000 Mumbai payment is not.",
  "starter": "import pandas as pd\n\ndef make_payments():\n    \"\"\"Sample UPI payments used by the checks - do not edit.\"\"\"\n    return pd.DataFrame({\n        \"city\": [\"Mumbai\"] * 6 + [\"Indore\"] * 6,\n        \"amount\": [4800, 5200, 5000, 5100, 4900, 20000,\n                   300, 350, 280, 320, 5000, 310],\n    })\n\ndef iqr_bounds(values):\n    \"\"\"Return (lower, upper) limits of the IQR rule for a pandas Series.\"\"\"\n    # TODO: q1, q3 = values.quantile(0.25), values.quantile(0.75)\n    pass\n\ndef outliers_by_group(df, group_col, value_col):\n    \"\"\"Rows that are outliers inside their own group.\"\"\"\n    # TODO 1: write a small function is_outlier(s) that returns a True/False Series for one group\n    # TODO 2: mask = df.groupby(group_col)[value_col].transform(is_outlier)\n    # TODO 3: return df[mask]\n    pass\n\n# Try it\npayments = make_payments()\nprint(iqr_bounds(payments[\"amount\"]))   # bounds for ALL cities together\nprint(payments.groupby(\"city\")[\"amount\"].describe())\n",
  "solution": "import pandas as pd\n\ndef make_payments():\n    \"\"\"Sample UPI payments used by the checks - do not edit.\"\"\"\n    return pd.DataFrame({\n        \"city\": [\"Mumbai\"] * 6 + [\"Indore\"] * 6,\n        \"amount\": [4800, 5200, 5000, 5100, 4900, 20000,\n                   300, 350, 280, 320, 5000, 310],\n    })\n\ndef iqr_bounds(values):\n    \"\"\"Return (lower, upper) limits of the IQR rule for a pandas Series.\"\"\"\n    q1, q3 = values.quantile(0.25), values.quantile(0.75)\n    iqr = q3 - q1\n    return (float(q1 - 1.5 * iqr), float(q3 + 1.5 * iqr))\n\ndef outliers_by_group(df, group_col, value_col):\n    \"\"\"Rows that are outliers inside their own group.\"\"\"\n    def is_outlier(s):                       # s holds the values of ONE group\n        lower, upper = iqr_bounds(s)\n        return (s < lower) | (s > upper)\n    mask = df.groupby(group_col)[value_col].transform(is_outlier).astype(bool)\n    return df[mask]\n\n# Try it\npayments = make_payments()\nprint(iqr_bounds(payments[\"amount\"]))   # bounds for ALL cities together\nprint(outliers_by_group(payments, \"city\", \"amount\"))   # rows 5 and 10\n",
  "tests": "check(\"iqr_bounds([10, 20, 30, 40, 1000]) is (-10.0, 70.0)\", lambda: close(iqr_bounds(pd.Series([10, 20, 30, 40, 1000])), (-10.0, 70.0)), \"Q1 = 20 and Q3 = 40, so IQR = 20. Lower = 20 - 30, upper = 40 + 30.\")\ncheck(\"iqr_bounds returns a (lower, upper) pair with lower < upper\", lambda: (lambda b: len(b) == 2 and b[0] < b[1])(iqr_bounds(pd.Series([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]))), \"Return a tuple: (q1 - 1.5 * iqr, q3 + 1.5 * iqr).\")\ncheck(\"outliers_by_group finds exactly rows 5 and 10 in make_payments()\", lambda: sorted(outliers_by_group(make_payments(), 'city', 'amount').index.tolist()) == [5, 10], \"Use groupby('city')['amount'].transform(is_outlier) to get one True/False per row, then df[mask].\")\ncheck(\"Indore's ₹5,000 is flagged, even though ₹5,000 is normal in Mumbai\", lambda: (lambda r: ('Indore', 5000) in list(zip(r['city'], r['amount'])) and ('Mumbai', 5000) not in list(zip(r['city'], r['amount'])))(outliers_by_group(make_payments(), 'city', 'amount')), \"The bounds must be computed separately for each city - that is what groupby does.\")\ncheck(\"the result keeps all the original columns\", lambda: list(outliers_by_group(make_payments(), 'city', 'amount').columns) == ['city', 'amount'], \"Filter rows with df[mask]; do not select only the value column.\")\ncheck(\"works with other column names: monsoon rain by state\", lambda: outliers_by_group(pd.DataFrame({'state': ['Kerala'] * 6 + ['Rajasthan'] * 6, 'rain_mm': [300, 320, 310, 290, 305, 900, 20, 25, 22, 18, 24, 21]}), 'state', 'rain_mm').index.tolist() == [5], \"Use the group_col and value_col arguments, not the fixed names 'city' and 'amount'.\")\ncheck(\"no outliers gives an empty DataFrame\", lambda: len(outliers_by_group(pd.DataFrame({'g': ['a'] * 4, 'v': [1, 2, 3, 4]}), 'g', 'v')) == 0, \"If the mask is all False, df[mask] has 0 rows.\")",
